  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$",
  "worked_solution": "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane.",
